<html>
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Clay Soil</title>

    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/backend.css">
</head>

<body>

<div class="page">
    <%- include("partials/header") %>

    <div class="main">
        <%- include("partials/sidebar") %>
        
        <section class="blackbox">

            <h1>🌱 Clay Soil – Summary</h1>
            <br>
            <img src="https://tse4.mm.bing.net/th/id/OIP.Wf4vObAUrXJ9gLtkYRp_1wHaFc?pid=Api&P=0&h=180" alt="clay soil">

            <p>
                Clay soil is made up of very fine particles that stick closely together.
                Because of this, it holds water and nutrients very well, but drainage and aeration are poor.
                It becomes sticky when wet and hard and cracked when dry.
            </p>

            <p>
                Clay soil is highly fertile but difficult to manage without proper practices
                like drainage and organic matter addition.
            </p>

            <h2>📊 Required Data about Clay Soil</h2>

            <b>1️⃣ Physical Properties</b>
            <ul>
                <li>Particle size: Very fine</li>
                <li>Texture: Smooth & sticky (wet), hard (dry)</li>
                <li>Color: Brown, red, black, or grey</li>
                <li>Water holding capacity: Very high</li>
                <li>Drainage: Poor</li>
                <li>Aeration: Low</li>
            </ul>

            <br>

            <b>2️⃣ Chemical Properties</b>
            <ul>
                <li>Nutrient content: High</li>
                <li>Fertility: High</li>
                <li>pH value: Mostly neutral to alkaline (7–8)</li>
                <li>Organic matter: Medium to high</li>
            </ul>

            <br>

            <b>3️⃣ Advantages</b>
            <ul>
                <li>Stores nutrients well</li>
                <li>Suitable for many crops</li>
                <li>Less frequent irrigation needed</li>
                <li>High yield when managed properly</li>
            </ul>

            <br>

            <b>4️⃣ Disadvantages</b>
            <ul>
                <li>Poor drainage</li>
                <li>Difficult to plough</li>
                <li>Waterlogging problem</li>
                <li>Hard surface in dry season</li>
            </ul>

            <br>

            <b>5️⃣ Suitable Crops for Clay Soil</b>
            <ul>
                <li>Rice</li>
                <li>Wheat</li>
                <li>Cotton</li>
                <li>Sugarcane</li>
                <li>Soybean</li>
                <li>Pulses</li>
            </ul>

            <br>

            <b>6️⃣ Management Practices</b>
            <ul>
                <li>Add organic manure / compost</li>
                <li>Use proper drainage channels</li>
                <li>Avoid over-irrigation</li>
                <li>Do deep ploughing before sowing</li>
                <li>Add sand or gypsum (if soil is too heavy)</li>
            </ul>

            <br>

            <b>7️⃣ Uses of Clay Soil</b>
            <ul>
                <li>Agriculture (rice fields)</li>
                <li>Brick making</li>
                <li>Pottery and ceramics</li>
                <li>Construction work</li>
            </ul>

        </section>

    </div>

</div>

</body>
</html>
